572:| OD15 | <a id="od15"></a>G10: whole-owner freezing under a shared borrow stays. G19: no pass assumes `&mut` exclusivity, and the operational loan rules are defined now | T-G10 (keep the freeze) and T-G19 (the policy sentence) | exit 2i (the G10 decision record), so every deliverable; R-0496, R-0505 | decided 2026-10-29 by the owner. (a) Whole-owner freezing: a live borrow of any part of a value freezes the whole owner, and loans are keyed by the root binding (REF-11 as in the current compiler); this tightens OD2's part 2: at a call, two arguments that borrow the same root conflict whenever one is exclusive, even disjoint fields (`h(&mut a.f, &mut a.g)` is refused, as in the current compiler); OD1's "keyed by the root variable" stands. (b) The strict option: the language guarantees `&mut` exclusivity, which the checker enforces (OD2, OD15a), and no compiler pass or optimization may assume or exploit it until the checker's guarantee is proven in Lean (R-0499 with the Core semantics R-0496); until then the operational loan rules are defined now, G19 included: a pointer or alias derived from a loan is valid only while that loan lives. Now REF-11@3 (partial, bug 319, which OD23 adds) and REF-14 (pending, R-0496) **Canonical current root-based exclusivity subject: REF-11 (including OD23’s re-lent case); OD2 and OD23 refer here rather than defining separate variants.** |
600:| OD43 | <a id="od43"></a>How a type position resolves a name (added 2026-10-29, from the spec gap bug 229's ruling found). Options: (a) the current compiler's rules: type positions search the type namespace only, a user `enum Option` is always the builtin, and a parent sees its child's newtypes; (b) a user declaration shadows a builtin of the same name, with a warning; (c) a user type may not take a builtin type's name (refused). Recommendation: (c), which removes the shadowing question entirely; Austral reserves its builtin type names the same way Reviewed pick (2026-10-30, a recommendation that supersedes the recommendation above where they differ): (c) refused, for builtin-name collisions only, with the complete reserved set published (also for aliases and import renames); this answer would not retire OD43, whose type-versus-value lookup and child-module questions stay open in it. A user `struct Copy HeapArray` is treated as linear today | the current compiler follows (a) | MOD-1; bug 229's record | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): Refuse builtin-name collisions, publish reserved set. Completed 2026-11-02 by the owner: one namespace per module, and a child module's declarations only through `child::T` or an import ([decisions](decisions.md#decided-2026-11-02-namespace)) |
621:| OD64 | <a id="od64"></a>Raw pointers derived from a borrow (added 2026-10-30, item G19 of the owner picks of that day). Question: What a raw pointer derived from a borrow may do: whether it is valid only while the borrow lives, may escape the borrow block, may be passed to C, and how extern parameters say so. Options: (a) valid only while the borrow lives and never escaping it; (b) the operational loan rules of REF-14 only. Pick (the coordinator's pick after the Kimi, Codex and Grok review of 2026-10-30, a recommendation and not a decision): (a): no returning, no escaping storage (a local `let` inside the block stays allowed), no converting to an integer and no capturing in an outer `defer`; borrow-derived pointers go only to call-only `extern` parameters; C uses it only during a call (memory C keeps must come from an allocation with its own owner); `extern` parameters say call-only (the default) or kept; field pointers come from the same borrow of the whole owner; deferred calls in the block run before the borrow ends; provenance survives copies, offsets, field pointers, helpers and indirect calls; a shared borrow never yields write access; `ptr::expose` is refused on such a pointer; the annotation's syntax gets an LL(1) check. Austral: not checked | no pass assumes `&mut` exclusivity (OD15); REF-14 bounds aliases by their loan | REF-14, TRUST-1, TRUST-3, FFI-3, OWN-12 | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): Valid only inside the borrow; call-only extern params |
624:| OD67 | <a id="od67"></a>Locals that reuse a top-level name (added 2026-10-30, item review new 4 (SCOPE-4) of the owner picks of that day). Question: Whether a local binding may reuse the name of a top-level function, an import alias or an intrinsic (MEMORY_SEMANTICS §15.4 also lists constants). Options: (a) allowed, as today; (b) refused, as SCOPE-1 refuses shadowing a local. Pick (the coordinator's pick after the Kimi, Codex and Grok review of 2026-10-30, a recommendation and not a decision): none given (raised by the review). Austral: not checked | allowed | SCOPE-1, SCOPE-2, MOD-1 | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): Refuse |
651:| OD94 | <a id="od94"></a>Names in `with(...)` that are not capabilities (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing CALLABLE_VALUES and HANDLE_CAPABILITIES). Question: Whether a name in `with(...)` that is not a capability, a capability alias or a capability variable in scope is refused. Bears on TRUST-D1, TYPE-D1, TRUST-4. The current compiler: accepted as a capability of its own: `fn main() with(Foo) -> Int` compiles and runs, and a call to a function that requires `Foo` is refused only in a caller that does not list it (E0520). The compiler's `validCaps`, TRUST-4's rationale and [SAFETY.md](../legacy/docs/verification/SAFETY.md) name nine capabilities. Options: (a) refuse an unknown name; (b) an open vocabulary. Austral: not applicable (capabilities are values). Pick: none given (raised by the absorption of 2026-10-30). | [MOD-1](../legacy/docs/spec/modules.md#MOD-1) refuses the name; the current compiler accepts it (bug 337) | MOD-1@1 | withdrawn 2026-10-30: not an owner question; MOD-1@1 refuses the name (bug 337) |

