codex
**Verdict: REJECT.** The stack analysis accepts understated bounds, and some failed controls cannot fail the gate.

1. **P1 — AArch64 post-index writeback on x29 silently understates the bound.** `scripts/checks/lib/stack_bound.py:166` rejects only pre-index `[x29, #k]!`; `a64_step` never updates x29 for post-index addressing. I reproduced acceptance of:
   ```asm
   mov x29, sp
   str x0, [x29], #-256
   str x0, [x29]
   add x29, x29, #256
   ret
   ```
   The analyzer returns **bound 0**, although the second store addresses 256 bytes below entry SP. LLVM 21’s disassembler emits this syntax. Reject both forms of x29 writeback, or model them.

2. **P1 — Unrecognized control-flow instructions become ordinary fallthrough.** `scripts/checks/lib/stack_bound.py:238` returns no control classification for x86 `loop`, `loope`, and `loopne`; the analysis then advances to the next instruction at line 301. A synthetic `mov ecx,2; loop deep; ret; deep: sub rsp,256; add rsp,256; ret` returns **bound 0**, missing the reachable 256-byte frame. The default acceptance of unknown instructions contradicts the fail-closed claim. Explicitly whitelist supported instruction semantics and reject unsupported control transfers.

3. **P1 — Refusal-control failures are lost in pipeline subshells.** `scripts/checks/slice0_artifacts.sh:180` through line 186 invoke `refuse` as the last pipeline component. Under normal Bash settings, `fail=1` at line 175 changes only the subshell. The failure branch’s assignment succeeds, so `pipefail` does not rescue this: the script can print `FAIL control` and ultimately exit zero. Run these controls through redirection/here-strings in the parent shell, or propagate a nonzero status explicitly.

4. **P1 — Abnormal termination can produce an accepted measurement.** `scripts/checks/lib/stack_measure.py:18` treats every inferior exit alike, and line 53 prints a measurement without checking the exit reason, expected status, or successful completion. For example, after accumulating a positive depth, an inferior can terminate from a fatal signal during external-call skipping and still produce `measured N`. `slice0_artifacts.sh:76` accepts that number whenever it fits the bound. This checks a possibly truncated execution. Require an expected termination outcome and validate debugger stops; also verify that execution actually reached the entry breakpoint before deriving the PIE offset.

5. **P2 — The shared memory cap is now escapable by every descendant.** `scripts/checks/lib/limits.sh:21` leaves the hard address-space limit unchanged. Any unprivileged child can raise its soft limit to that hard limit; `limited_exec.sh 0` has no exclusive ability to do so. I confirmed a shell inheriting `gate_limits 1 ...` could restore its soft limit to `unlimited`. The “only limited_exec.sh 0” claim at lines 11–13 is false. Scope this exception to the sanitizer execution path rather than weakening the shared gate limit.

6. **P2 — README marks incomplete owner requirements as implemented.** `Concrete/README.md:582` calls gate 10 implemented while acknowledging that UBSan checks nothing; line 584 similarly calls gate 12 implemented while skipping the run check on macOS. Those qualifications are candid, but they do not satisfy the stated sanitizer and artifact-versus-run obligations. Mark the missing coverage explicitly as incomplete, or obtain an owner-approved scope change.

Adding `sanitize_address` is appropriate for enabling ASan instrumentation on these IR definitions; it does not supply UBSan checks. LLVM documents it as the ASan function attribute. [LLVM attribute definition](https://github.com/llvm/llvm-project/blob/main/llvm/include/llvm/IR/Attributes.td)

Validation was limited to source inspection, in-memory analyzer reproducers, an LLVM disassembly probe, and shell behavior checks. I did not rerun the full gate suite or modify files. No independent second reviewer was used.
